= ψ Impedance Theory =

ψ impedance theory answers the question every device engineer must ask after specifying a source: how much of the emitted ψ actually couples into the field, and how much is wasted? In electromagnetic engineering this question gave rise to transmission line theory, the Smith chart, and conjugate matching — among the most practically useful tools in all of engineering. The ψ equivalent is derived here from the same first principles.

The central result is that ψ wave impedance is '''frequency-dependent''' — unlike the constant 377 Ω of electromagnetic free space. This has an immediate engineering consequence: a [[Psi Stabilizer]] designed for 40 kHz operating frequency has a fixed <math>Z_\psi^{\rm (device)}</math>, but the operator's biological ψ impedance <math>Z_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> varies with their physiological state. The Mk2.0 closed-loop mode exists precisely to track this variation and retune the device frequency to maintain the conjugate match.

