== Prerequisites ==

This page requires two results from earlier Branch A pages:

* From [[ψ Conservation Laws]] (A2): the ψ current density <math>\mathbf{J}_\psi = -(\partial_t\psi)\nabla\psi = \vec{S}_\psi</math> (ψ current equals ψ Poynting vector).
* From [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3): the dispersion relation <math>\omega^2 = k^2 + m_{\rm eff}^2</math> and the refractive index <math>n_\psi = k/\omega</math>.

== Definition of ψ Wave Impedance ==

=== From First Principles ===

For any wave field, impedance is defined as the ratio of the "force-like" quantity to the "flow-like" quantity — exactly as voltage-to-current ratio defines electrical impedance. For ψ waves, the natural pair is:

* '''Force-like:''' ψ field amplitude <math>\psi</math>
* '''Flow-like:''' ψ source current density <math>J_\psi^{\rm (src)}</math>

For a forward-propagating plane wave <math>\psi = A\,e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x} - \omega t)}</math>:

:<math>\partial_t\psi = -i\omega A\,e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}-\omega t)}</math>
:<math>\partial_x\psi = ik_x A\,e^{i(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}-\omega t)}</math>

The ψ Poynting vector (= ψ current density from A2) in the propagation direction <math>\hat{x}</math>:

:<math>S_\psi^x = -(\partial_t\psi)(\partial_x\psi) = -(-i\omega)(ik_x)|A|^2 = -\omega k_x |A|^2</math>

Taking <math>k_x = k = \omega n_\psi</math> and the field amplitude as <math>\psi_{\rm amp} = A</math>:

:<math>Z_\psi \equiv \frac{\psi_{\rm amp}}{|S_\psi^x|/\psi_{\rm amp}} = \frac{A^2}{\omega k |A|^2} = \frac{1}{\omega k} = \frac{1}{\omega^2 n_\psi}</math>

:<math>\boxed{Z_\psi = \frac{1}{\omega^2 n_\psi} = \frac{1}{\omega\,k}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ wave impedance
| equation       = <math>Z_\psi = \frac{1}{\omega^2 n_\psi} = \frac{1}{\omega\sqrt{\omega^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2}}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>Z_\psi</math> — the ψ wave impedance. High impedance = field amplitude large relative to power flow (like a high-Q resonator). Low impedance = large power flow relative to field amplitude (like a broadband radiator).
* <math>\omega</math> — operating angular frequency. Higher frequency → lower <math>Z_\psi</math> → easier to drive.
* <math>n_\psi = k/\omega</math> — ψ refractive index of the medium. A denser medium (larger <math>n_\psi</math>) has lower impedance.
* <math>m_{\rm eff}</math> — effective mass from [[ψ Dispersion Relations]]. As <math>\omega \to \omega_c = m_{\rm eff}</math> (cutoff), <math>n_\psi \to 0</math> and <math>Z_\psi \to \infty</math> — impedance diverges at cutoff, consistent with the fact that no power propagates below cutoff.
* '''Key difference from EM impedance:''' The electromagnetic vacuum impedance <math>Z_{\rm EM} = 377\,\Omega</math> is a constant. The ψ vacuum impedance <math>Z_\psi = 1/\omega^2</math> depends on frequency — this is the defining engineering distinction.
}}

=== Impedance in a ψ Metamaterial Medium ===

In a medium with effective ψ permittivity <math>\varepsilon_\psi</math> and permeability <math>\mu_\psi</math> (defined formally in [[ψ Constitutive Relations]], B1):

:<math>Z_\psi^{\rm (medium)} = \frac{1}{\omega^2}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_\psi}{\varepsilon_\psi}}</math>

This is the generalization of the EM formula <math>Z_{\rm EM} = \sqrt{\mu/\varepsilon}</math>, modified by the additional <math>1/\omega^2</math> factor arising from the wave equation structure. Engineering the ratio <math>\mu_\psi/\varepsilon_\psi</math> is the primary lever for impedance matching in [[ψ Metamaterials]].

== Reflection, Transmission, and Matching ==

=== At a Material Boundary ===

At a planar boundary between medium 1 (impedance <math>Z_1</math>) and medium 2 (impedance <math>Z_2</math>), continuity of ψ and its normal derivative gives the Fresnel-like coefficients:

:<math>\Gamma = \frac{Z_2 - Z_1}{Z_2 + Z_1} \qquad \text{(reflection coefficient)}</math>

:<math>T_\psi = \frac{2Z_2}{Z_2 + Z_1} \qquad \text{(field transmission coefficient)}</math>

Power reflection and transmission:

:<math>R = |\Gamma|^2 \qquad \text{(fraction of power reflected)}</math>

:<math>\tau = 1 - R = \frac{4Z_1 Z_2}{(Z_1 + Z_2)^2} \qquad \text{(fraction of power transmitted)}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ power transmission coefficient
| equation       = <math>\tau = \frac{4Z_1 Z_2}{(Z_1 + Z_2)^2}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>\tau = 1</math> when <math>Z_1 = Z_2</math> — perfect impedance match, all power transmitted, none reflected.
* <math>\tau \to 0</math> when <math>Z_2 \gg Z_1</math> or <math>Z_2 \ll Z_1</math> — large mismatch, most power reflected.
* The function is symmetric in <math>Z_1</math> and <math>Z_2</math>: it does not matter which medium is "denser" — only the ratio matters.
* '''Engineering design equation:''' for the [[Psi Stabilizer]] to transfer more than 80% of its emitted ψ power into the operator's biological field, the device impedance must satisfy <math>0.5 < Z_{\rm device}/Z_{\rm bio} < 2</math> (from inverting the formula for <math>\tau > 0.8</math>). This is the tolerance budget for the impedance matching design.
}}

=== The Conjugate Matching Condition ===

When sources and loads have complex impedances (resistive + reactive components), maximum power transfer occurs at:

:<math>Z_{\rm device} = Z^*_{\rm bio} \qquad \text{(complex conjugate match)}</math>

The biological ψ impedance <math>Z_{\rm bio}</math> has a reactive component because the operator's cortical ψ oscillation is a damped resonance — not a pure resistive load. The optimal device impedance is the complex conjugate: same magnitude, opposite reactive sign. Physically this means the device should be tuned to the conjugate of the operator's resonance, injecting ψ at exactly the frequency and phase that maximizes energy absorption.

== The Mk1.0 / Mk2.0 Engineering Distinction ==

The impedance matching requirement is the rigorous mathematical foundation of the distinction between [[HelmKit Mk1.0]] and [[HelmKit Mk2.0]].

